===Etymology===
Believed to be from a quotation by the British-Irish statesman and philosopher {{w|Edmund Burke}} (1729–1797): “Falsehood and delusion are allowed in no case whatever: but, as in the exercise of all the virtues, there is an œconomy of truth. It is a sort of temperance, by which a man speaks truth with measure that he may speak it the longer.”<ref>{{cite-book|author=Edmund Burke|authorlink=Edmund Burke|chapter=Competence, Circumstances, Prudence|title=Maxims and Opinions, Moral, Political, and Economical, with Characters, from the Works of the Right Hon. Edmund Burke|location=London|publisher=Printed by [[w:Charles Whittingham|C[harles] Whittingham]], {{w|Dean Street}}, {{w|Fetter Lane}}; for T. Ostel, [[w:Ave Maria Lane|Ave-Maria-Lane]]|year=1804|volume=I|pages=54–55|oclc=15870049}}.</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{hyphenation|eco|no|mi|cal with the truth|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{head|en|adjective}}

# {{label|en|idiomatic|euphemistic}} Not [[telling]] the [[whole]] truth, especially in order to present a [[false]] [[image]] of a situation; [[untruthful]]; [[lying]]. {{n-g|Often used with [[sarcasm]] or [[satire]].}}
#: {{ux|en|I would be being '''economical with the truth''' if I were to tell you that I was enjoying myself.}}

